Looking at the `entire.ts` plugin (which definitely works), I can see on line 167:
```typescript
if (msg.role === "assistant" && msg.modelID) {
  currentModel = msg.modelID
}
```

So the `entire.ts` plugin also only reads `msg.modelID`, not `msg.providerID`. This suggests `providerID` might not be available in the event data, or it's stored differently.

Wait - but the `AssistantMessage` type definition shows `providerID` as a flat field. But maybe in the ACTUAL event data that reaches the plugin, `providerID` is not present.

Let me look at how the event bus serializes messages. The event might be using a different mechanism that doesn't include all fields.

Actually, I just realized something important: looking at `EventMessageUpdated`:
```typescript
export type EventMessageUpdated = {
    type: "message.updated";
    properties: {
        sessionID: string;
        info: Message;
    };
};
```

This is the TYPED event. But the plugin receives the ACTUAL event which might be serialized differently. The plugin bridge might serialize the event as JSON and deserialize it on the other side. If the internal representation uses different field names or drops some fields, the plugin would see a different shape.

The safest approach is to not rely on `providerID` at all and derive it from the `modelID` itself. Many model IDs follow a pattern like `provider/model` or `provider:model`. 

But actually, looking at the model IDs in our data:
- `deepseek-v4-flash-free`

This doesn't follow a `provider/model` pattern. The provider could be "deepseek" based on the model name prefix.

Or maybe I should look at how OpenCode resolves provider from model. Let me check the config...

Actually, the simplest fix is to use a fallback for the provider. If `info.providerID` is not available, I can try to extract it from the modelID (first part before `-`), or just use an empty string.

But actually, the user specifically wants `model.id` and `model.provider` decomposed. If `providerID` is not available in the event, maybe I should get it from the modelID by parsing it, or use a different field.

Wait, let me re-read the user's request: "the model nice to have decomposed into id & providerID (somany providers providing same model)"

Maybe I should check if the model info is available from the session config or from the OpenCode config. The provider ID might be stored as part of the model configuration.

Actually, let me take a step back. The user said "still model id only" - meaning the YAML output is still `model: "deepseek-v4-flash-free"` as a flat string, not `model: { id: "...", provider: "..." }`.

But my code change converts `model` from a string to a `ModelInfo` object. If the code is compiled and loaded correctly, the YAML builder would render it as a nested object.

Unless `modelFromInfo` returns `null` for all cases, and the YAML uses a default empty string. But I check `if (title) header.title = title` which only adds optional fields. The `model` is always included in the header.

Wait - looking at the header construction:
```typescript
const header: Record<string, unknown> = {
  session: { id: ses, created: ..., updated: ... },
  model,
}
```

`model` is `ModelInfo | null`. If `modelFromInfo` returns null, `model` is null, and `yamlFields` skips null values. So the YAML header wouldn't have a `model` field at all! But the user says "model id only" - meaning it IS there but as a string.

So `model` must be a string at runtime. This means the code change didn't take effect. The old version of the plugin (with `model: string | null`) is still running.

This means the user hasn't restarted opencode since my last changes.

Let me just ask the user to restart opencode.

